{
  "schema": "rnk.sequence-encoding-vector.v1",
  "about": "The normative byte-level encoding vector for the RNK sequence certificate and logs tree. Forty cases. Every digest here is transcribed from the pinned construction document, never regenerated; a digest that appears to have moved is a finding, not a value to update. Two repositories compute these hashes: reactive-tornado (Go, the consensus side) and the Sequencer's certificate issuer (TypeScript). This file is the only artifact either side may treat as normative, and it is fetched, never copied.",
  "sourceDocument": "abci/rnk/docs/sequence-encoding-construction.md, revision 4 (2026-08-21)",
  "caseCount": 40,
  "layout": {
    "leaf": "leafTag(14 raw ASCII) | seqNumber(8 BE) | status(8 BE) | txHash(32) | address(20) | nTopics(4 BE) | topics(32 each) | lenData(4 BE) | data - total 90 + 32*nTopics + lenData bytes. The log INDEX is not a field.",
    "node": "nodeTag(14 raw ASCII) | left(32) | right(32) - 78 bytes",
    "cert": "certTag(14 raw ASCII) | NetworkId(8 BE) | SeqNumber(8 BE) | Kind(1) | KeyId(4 BE) | ChainId(8 BE) | BlockHash(32) | ParentHash(32) | Number(8 BE) | Time(8 BE) | LogCount(4 BE) | LogsRoot(32) - 159 bytes, always",
    "tree": "RFC 6962 section 2.1, both base cases: MTH({}) = keccak256(\"\"); MTH({d0}) = leaf(d0); MTH(D[n]) = node(MTH(D[0:k]), MTH(D[k:n])) with k the largest power of two STRICTLY below n. No padding; n need not be a power of two. No interior node is applied at n = 1 and no root tag is applied.",
    "proof": "Deduplicated RFC 6962 section 2.1.1 audit paths. Node identity is the leaf interval [lo, hi), never (level, index). The table is transmitted in ascending canonical interval order (lo then hi); the verifier regenerates that list from LogCount and the carried indices and folds each leaf in RFC 6962's own order, which is NOT the table order.",
    "signature": "secp256k1 over certDigest, 65 bytes r || s || v, low-S, and v is the recovery id 0 or 1 - NOT the {27,28} most Ethereum tooling emits.",
    "integers": "Every integer is a fixed-width big-endian field. No RLP, protobuf or JSON appears inside a hashed preimage. Decimal integers in this file are STRINGS so that no parser has to hold 2^64-1 as a float."
  },
